Sval: a Peek opens no sidebar

A Peek is a full browser window that holds one page from another
app, in no workspace (SvalPeek). SidebarController starts a window
with no opener from the state the last browser window to close left
(sidebar.backupState, in startDelayedLoad), and a link from another app
opens its Peek with no opener, so the Peek opened whatever
sidebar that window had open. Firefox's taskbar tab window, which also holds
one page, is kept out of every sidebar by inSingleTabWindow: it adopts no
state, show() and showInitially() refuse, the sidebar menu's items are
disabled, and getUIState() gives no state to save as the backup. A Peek
takes the same answer.

diff --git a/browser/components/sidebar/browser-sidebar.js b/browser/components/sidebar/browser-sidebar.js
index 947f026af9..c03556fb32 100644
--- a/browser/components/sidebar/browser-sidebar.js
+++ b/browser/components/sidebar/browser-sidebar.js
@@ -366,7 +366,9 @@ var SidebarController = {
   get inSingleTabWindow() {
     return (
       !window.toolbar.visible ||
-      window.document.documentElement.hasAttribute("taskbartab")
+      window.document.documentElement.hasAttribute("taskbartab") ||
+      // Sval: a Peek holds one page, as a taskbar tab does.
+      window.document.documentElement.hasAttribute("svalpeek")
     );
   },
 
